You are writing AgentMark, a general-purpose programming language written as Markdown: files ending in .agent.md that `agentmark build` compiles to a Rust binary (records, enums, functions, commands, services, collection words, a built-in library, files). Agents that ask an AI model are one feature of it. You are not writing Rust.

SOURCE OF TRUTH
Read docs/reference.md of the AgentMark source (~/.agentmark/src after install.sh) before writing anything. It is the whole language in one file: every declaration, step form and error message, with tested examples. If it does not show a construct, the construct does not exist; ask the human instead of inventing syntax. If this project lives outside the AgentMark source, keep a copy of that file next to the project and read that.

WORKFLOW
1. Edit one .agent.md file.
2. Run `agentmark check .` right after every edit and fix every error before doing anything else. The messages say what is wrong and often what to write.
3. Run `agentmark fmt .` (canonical style; `agentmark fmt --check .` only reports).
4. Run `agentmark lint .` and fix the warnings.
5. Run and test: `agentmark run . -- <arguments>` or `agentmark trace . -- <arguments>` (the arguments after `--` go to the program). A program with a model or outside tools adds `--mock --fixtures fixtures/<name>`. Compare output and traces, not generated Rust.
6. `agentmark doc .` prints the description and what each command, function, agent and block touches; `agentmark check .` prints the same "What each part touches" report. Read it after every change.

RULES
- Never write Rust, except inside a `## Rust: <name>` block the human asked for. Never edit generated code (target/ or an -o output folder); change the .agent.md source.
- Do not edit rust/connect.rs beyond the tool implementations the human asked for.
- The `- effect:` line in bindings/*.agent.md is for humans to approve. Never change `- effect: unverified` there, and never loosen a block's tools to silence `check`. Show the human what the library can touch and let them approve it. --allow-unverified is for exploring, not for finishing.
- A `## Rust` block needs a `- touches:` line. Declare what it honestly touches (nothing, files, print, AI or a tool name; never less to pass `check`) and tell the human, because that line is trusted, not verified. A function or tool needs no touches line: `check` works it out, and a line you write is verified.
- One step per line, at most 120 characters, at most 3 levels of nesting. Write a description (a plain paragraph directly under the heading) for every part: record, enum, function, command, tool, rule, agent, trigger and `## Do` block. A `>` line is only a note and never a description; a `>` directly above a heading is an error.
- Steps are a numbered list (`1.` everywhere is fine; fmt renumbers), properties are bullets. Name a result with `into`: `group sales by region into by_region`, `try file read path into sales`. These old forms are errors: `set`, `->` after a step, `append`, `args:`, `## Run`, `# Run`, `- for:` in a rule, `# Import: ./file`, `- effect:` on a function. The messages show the replacement.
- A rule names its record last in the heading: `# Rule: big Sale`. Add to a list with `add RegionTotal to totals:` and one bullet per field. The entry file starts with `# Program: <name>`, a description and `- start: [command](#command-name)`, then a `# Uses` list of links like `- [Sale](./records/sale.agent.md)`.
- In an agent, follow every `ask model` with a `guard:` on its answer. Name a money, duration or URL literal used more than once as a `# Constant`.
- Tools outside the program come from tools.toml; do not use a tool or verb that is not listed there. The `file` tool and the library (text, regex, number, money, date, path, json, csv) are built in. A helper you can write in AgentMark is a `# Tool` or `# Function`.
- No secrets in files; keys come from the environment (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY).

WHERE THINGS LIVE
agentmark.toml (name, version, targets, [lint], [models], [crates] for library features, [rust.dependencies]); tools.toml (tool manifest; it and library bindings keep the word effect); main.agent.md (`# Program`, `- start:` and the `# Uses` links); records/, functions/, commands/, services/, rules/, agents/ (one declaration per file); bindings/ (generated by `agentmark bind`, reviewed by a human); fixtures/ (model answers, tool data, expected traces; for a program that is not an agent run.json, input/ and expected.stdout); rust/connect.rs (real tool implementations). A new command, service or agent is a file plus a link in the `# Uses` list of main.agent.md (and `- start:` for the command the program starts); a program with exactly one command and no agent or service is started as `my-tool <arguments>`.

If you are stuck, say what you tried and what `check` answered. Do not work around the checker.
